      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, weapons and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Style: heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and fabric, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. Scene: the camera stands at eye level in the middle of a stalled car queue at a bridge checkpoint, late afternoon, the air brown with dust and exhaust. An enormous Russian tricolour — horizontal white, blue and red bands — fills the left two-thirds of the background from top to bottom, hanging heavy and slack from the checkpoint gantry, painted in thick impasto, edges scorched, soot ground into the deep folds, the colours sun-bleached and dull. In front of it a column of stranded civilian drivers recedes into the frame beside a line of cars that runs unbroken to the horizon: the nearest, a Russian woman in her forties in a thin jacket, is framed waist-up just left of centre and rendered sharp, while everyone behind her grows progressively smaller and hazier in the exhaust haze. Her eyes are half-lidded and dulled, her mouth open in a slow resigned exhale, shoulders sunk, sweat shining at her temple, one h