      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, weapons and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Style: heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and fabric, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. Scene: the camera sits low, at the level of a narrow pontoon crossing laid across a shallow lagoon, looking along the crossing so that a single file of Russian military supply trucks runs away from the viewer into the haze, bumper to bumper and completely stopped, water slopping over the joints of the floating sections. An enormous Russian tricolour — horizontal white, blue and red bands — fills the entire left half of the frame from top to bottom, strung between two wrecked pylons, its heavy cloth thick with impasto brushwork, the lower edge charred and torn into ragged strips, soot staining every fold, the colours dull and muted under raking orange firelight. In front of it a group of Russian soldiers stands blocking the crossing: the nearest, a man in his thirties in a soaked flak vest, is framed waist-up at left of centre and painted sharp, one hand hooked through his rifle sling at chest height, jaw 